      "sequence_position": 18,
      "problem": "Let $I=[0,1]⊂R$ and ${X}_{i}=\\{i\\}\\times [0,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi i)].$ Which of the following represents ${⋂}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that the intersection of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *all* the sets:\n\n$$\n{⋂}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}=\\{x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for all}\\,\\,k\\in I\\}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=\\frac{1}{2},$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{1/2}=\\{\\frac{1}{2}\\}\\times [0,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})]=\\{\\frac{1}{2}\\}\\times [0,1]=\\{(\\frac{1}{2},k)\\,:\\,k\\in [0,1]\\}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a vertical line segment from the point $(\\frac{1}{2},0)$ to the point $(\\frac{1}{2},1)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14242415/assets/2827c5b33e84aff775d8bcc7ca98492afb220dda3a52fec4166afaa4da187d66.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{i\\}\\times [0,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi i)]=\\{(i,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi k))\\,:\\,k\\in [0,i]\\}\n$$\n\nis the vertical line segment from the point $(i,0)$ to the point $(i,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi i)).$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14242415/assets/5a108a3c0cdf37a072d135674b6ffdb3894b8c5ba142604ea7a434b0f42245f5.png)\n\nThere are no points that lie in all sets. Therefore, the intersection of the indexed sets is $∅,$ the empty set.",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
